    "body": "LeoLabs' new Scout-S fits in a standardized 20-foot container for easy deployment. (Photo: LeoLabs)\nWASHINGTON — Space tracking firm LeoLabs today announced it has acquired FishEye Software as it looks to expand its missile defense footprint.\n“As a wholly owned subsidiary of LeoLabs, FishEye will strengthen our ability to accelerate delivery of our next-generation radars optimized for missile warning and tracking, such as Scout-X ,” a LeoLabs spokesperson told Breaking Defense.\nThe highly mobile Scout-X made its debut in June during during US Indo-Pacific Command’s multinational Valiant Shield exercise. The radar fits in a standard shipping container and can detect both satellites and missiles flying at altitudes of about 230 to 1,000 kilometers (143 miles to 621 miles) above the Earth.\nFounded in 1997 and headquartered in Massachusetts, FishEye has a pedigree in supporting the Pentagon’s missile defense programs. It is one of several thousand small firms chosen last December for the Missile Defense Agency’s Scalable Homeland Innovative Enterprise Layered Defense ( SHIELD ) contract vehicle, which is worth up to $151 billion through 2035, and supports the Trump administration’s Golden Dome initiative.\n“FishEye brings decades of experience developing and integrating software for U.S. missile defense systems,” LeoLabs CEO Tony Frazier said in a statement. “That expertise will help us continue to adapt our proven radar technology for missile warning and tracking, and move those capabilities into operational use quickly.”\nWhile the LeoLabs announcement did not reveal the price tag of the acquisition, FishEye’s revenue is projected to reach approximately $11.9 million this year, according to corporate research website RocketReach.\nBreaking Defense Video\nNATO nations have a missile crisis. How did it happen, and can it be fixed?\nWatch Now »\nA Pentagon impeachment showdown, plus defense funding in limbo\nWatch Now »\npresented by\nThe push to give dismounted forces direct access to LEO and MEO networks [Sponsored]\nWatch Now »\nThe Pentagon wants a massive new military base. Where will it go?\nWatch Now »\nWhat now? Budget fight, Hegseth impeachment on deck for defense-focused lawmakers after midterms\nWatch Now »\nThe defense show circuit keeps rolling, plus Jimmy Stewart goes to war\nWatch Now »\nQatar’s domestic weapons, Turkey’s drone arsenal and the Mecca pact’s next test\nWatch Now »\n3 congressional races that matter for defense\nWatch Now »\nHow General Atomics views Europe’s unmanned future\nWatch Now »\npresented by\nA more flexible way to defend maneuver forces from drones that doesn’t tie them down [Sponsored]\nWatch Now »\nScroll for more video",
